```lean
/-! ## 4. The ball-measure realization interface -/

/-- **Class:** metric–measure interface (non-manifold).

**Interface predicate (a definition, not an assumed theorem).**  A measure `μ` on a
pseudo-metric space `X` *realizes the radial volume profile* `A : ℝ → ℝ` if every closed
ball, at every centre and every (real) radius, has `μ`-measure equal to the radial volume
`radialVolume A s = ∫₀ˢ A`.

This is exactly the additional hypothesis that makes `radialVolume A` a genuine ball
measure.  It is deliberately *not* claimed for any manifold, Riemannian metric, or
curvature bound: it is the hypothesis under which the round-3 measure-growth theorems
(`coveringNumber_le_measure_ratio`, `coveringNumber_le_of_measure_doubling`,
`coveringNumber_le_of_dyadic_doubling`) apply with `μ (closedBall x s) = radialVolume A s`.
The `ENNReal.ofReal` coercion is the canonical map `ℝ → ℝ≥0∞` (negatives are truncated
to `0`), so the identity is required for all real radii, including negative ones (both
sides are then `0`).

A genuine Riemannian ball measure would only satisfy this after (i) multiplying by the
angular constant `ω_{n−1}` of the unit sphere, and (ii) proving centre-independence of the
ball volume profile; neither is available in this project (see the gap section below). -/
def IsRadialBallMeasure {X : Type*} [PseudoMetricSpace X] (μ : Measure X) (A : ℝ → ℝ) : Prop :=
  ∀ (x : X) (s : ℝ), μ (closedBall x s) = ENNReal.ofReal (radialVolume A s)
```

